# /etc/xbrain/robot.env -- TEMPLATE. Copy to /etc/xbrain/robot.env and edit.
#
# Per-robot identity: the L5 environment layer (11 S1.1.9.7 whitelist). These are
# the ONLY env keys the config loader (xbrain/common/config/layers.py) accepts;
# anything else set in a runtime process's environment makes config-freeze abort.
#
# WHO READS THIS
#   - rtk_driver  (ros2_ws/sensor/src/main.cc): XBRAIN_ROBOT_ID is REQUIRED with no
#     default -- the process exits at startup if it is unset. That is deliberate
#     fail-stop: a driver with no rid would publish rt/gnss/* under the wrong key.
#   - p1_motion   (xbrain/p1_motion/runtime/main_wiring.py): the gnss->state/pose
#     bridge is SKIPPED (logs "p1 gnss bridge OFF: XBRAIN_ROBOT_ID unset") when the
#     var is absent, so state/pose goes silent.
#   - config-freeze reads common.robot_id / common.site_id from the RESOLVED snapshot,
#     NOT from this file at runtime. This file is the source the L5 layer reads when
#     the snapshot is built.
#
# WHY XBRAIN_ROBOT_ID IS LEFT UNSET BELOW
#   Shipping a placeholder rid (e.g. CHANGE-ME) would let rtk_driver + p1 start and
#   publish under a bogus id -- fail-silent. Leaving it unset makes rtk_driver refuse
#   to start with a clear message until a real rid is written here. Set it, then start.
#
# systemd EnvironmentFile syntax: KEY=value, one per line, no `export`, no surrounding
# quotes. Referenced as EnvironmentFile=-/etc/xbrain/robot.env (the leading - means the
# unit still starts if the file is absent; rtk_driver then fail-stops on the missing rid).

# --- REQUIRED: uncomment and set to this robot's id before first start ---
# XBRAIN_ROBOT_ID=m20s-001

# --- OPTIONAL: site id (maps to common.site_id; used for site-local config) ---
# XBRAIN_SITE_ID=fire-station-a

# --- OPTIONAL: log level (DEBUG|INFO|WARNING|ERROR); unset -> process default ---
# XBRAIN_LOG_LEVEL=INFO
